<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#polonisation">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polonisation</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Polonization</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Polonisation is the adoption of the Polish language, culture and often Catholic religion by non-Polish groups. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> it affected above all the Lithuanian and Ruthenian nobility after the unions with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and in 1696 Polish replaced Ruthenian as the official language of administration. The term is also used for state policies of the Second Polish Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polonisation.jpg" alt="Polonisation" width="320" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Krzysztoflew · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borders_of_Polish_states_and_Polish%E2%80%93Lithuanian_Commonwealth_(union_state_of_the_Crown_of_the_Kingdom_of_Poland_and_Grand_Duchy_of_Lithuania).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
